# Typology language: clear pattern, not identified

Crescent National Bank is filing this SAR on customer Linda Foster, age 72, of Boise, Idaho, regarding unusual outgoing wire activity through personal checking account 63917402. Foster opened the account on August 11, 2018. At account opening, Foster identified herself as retired and reported monthly income of approximately $4,800 from Social Security, pension payments, and investment distributions. The account has historically been used for household expenses, medical costs, charitable donations, credit card payments, and other routine personal activity.

From January 2024 through April 2026, Foster’s account activity was generally stable. She had no international wire activity and no domestic wires exceeding $5,000 during that period.

Between May 19, 2026, and August 14, 2026, Foster completed six outgoing wire transfers totaling $162,300 to five beneficiaries in the United States, Malaysia, and Hong Kong:

May 19: $18,500 to Alpine Administrative Services LLC at a U.S. financial institution.
June 3: $24,800 to Golden Harbor Trading Ltd. in Hong Kong.
June 21: $31,000 to Meridian Project Services LLC at a U.S. financial institution.
July 8: $27,500 to Golden Harbor Trading Ltd. in Hong Kong.
July 27: $26,500 to Eastern Meridian Holdings Ltd. in Malaysia.
August 14: $34,000 to Pacific Resource Consultants Ltd. in Hong Kong.

Foster funded the wires primarily through transfers from savings and investment accounts held in her own name. Between May 15 and August 12, 2026, she transferred approximately $175,000 into checking account 63917402. Historical review showed no comparable pattern of asset liquidation or large payments to unrelated third parties.

On May 19, 2026, Foster visited a Crescent National Bank branch in Boise to initiate the $18,500 wire to Alpine Administrative Services LLC. When asked about the purpose of the payment, Foster stated that she was helping a friend named “Robert” with a temporary business expense. Foster said she had met Robert through an online social-media platform approximately three months earlier.

Foster stated that Robert was an American engineer working overseas and had experienced difficulty accessing funds related to a construction project. Foster said Robert intended to repay her when the project was completed. She stated that she had never met Robert in person but communicated with him daily through text messages, email, and video calls. Foster described the relationship as romantic.

On June 3, 2026, Foster sent $24,800 to Golden Harbor Trading Ltd. in Hong Kong. She stated that Robert told her the payment was necessary to release construction equipment being held by an overseas supplier. Foster provided a photograph of an invoice referencing “equipment release fees,” but the document did not identify Foster as a purchaser, did not identify Robert by full legal name, and did not contain independently verifiable contact information.

Crescent National Bank personnel warned Foster that fraud schemes sometimes involve individuals met online who request money for emergencies, business expenses, taxes, customs fees, or similar obligations. Foster stated that she understood the concern but believed Robert was legitimate.

On June 21, 2026, Foster sent $31,000 to Meridian Project Services LLC. Foster stated that the payment represented legal and administrative expenses associated with Robert’s business project. She provided a one-page document titled “Project Settlement Authorization,” but the document did not identify a law firm, court, government agency, case number, or contract number.

Public-record review identified Meridian Project Services LLC as a Nevada limited liability company formed in February 2026 using a commercial registered-agent address. Crescent National Bank identified no apparent relationship between Foster and the company.

On July 8, 2026, Foster sent an additional $27,500 to Golden Harbor Trading Ltd. Foster stated that an additional customs-related payment was required before Robert’s equipment could be delivered.

During an enhanced review on July 15, 2026, Foster identified the individual as Robert Hayes, approximately 65 years old. Foster could not provide a verifiable employer, permanent address, or business registration associated with Robert.

Foster provided screenshots of messages exchanged with Robert. The messages contained affectionate language, discussion of a future relationship, and instructions concerning payments. In one message, Robert stated that Foster would be repaid in full after his project funds were released. In another, he stated that failure to make an additional payment could cause him to lose the project and the money already invested.

Crescent National Bank identified no independent documentation verifying Robert’s identity or the existence of the described overseas construction project.

On July 27, 2026, Foster sent $26,500 to Eastern Meridian Holdings Ltd. in Malaysia. Foster stated that Robert told her the payment represented taxes required before project proceeds could be released.

On August 7, 2026, Foster’s adult son contacted Crescent National Bank and stated that he was concerned his mother was sending significant amounts of money to someone she had met online. He stated that Foster had discussed plans to marry Robert after Robert returned to the United States and that no family member had met Robert in person. Foster’s son was not an owner or authorized signer on the account, and the bank did not disclose account information to him.

On August 14, 2026, Foster completed a $34,000 wire to Pacific Resource Consultants Ltd. in Hong Kong. Foster stated that Robert told her this was the final fee required before he could receive approximately $2 million in project proceeds and repay her.

Crescent National Bank considers the activity suspicious because Foster, a retired customer with no prior history of international wires or large third-party payments, transferred $162,300 to multiple previously unrelated domestic and foreign beneficiaries after developing an online relationship with an individual she had never met in person. The transactions were funded through substantial transfers from Foster’s savings and investment accounts and represented a significant change from her historical account activity.

Foster provided limited documentation supporting the stated purposes of the payments, and Crescent National Bank was unable to independently verify Robert’s identity, the purported construction project, or Foster’s relationship to the beneficiary companies. Foster continued sending increasingly large amounts despite fraud warnings from bank personnel and concerns expressed by a family member.

Crescent National Bank considers the transactions unusual and inconsistent with Foster’s historical account activity and stated customer profile. The bank has not established that Foster knowingly participated in criminal activity or that the named beneficiary companies knowingly received illicit funds.

Crescent National Bank restricted additional outgoing wire activity on August 14, 2026 and placed the account under enhanced review. The account remains open. Account statements, wire records, customer communications, screenshots provided by Foster, supporting documents, customer due diligence information, public-record research, and related investigative records are available upon request.
